Current state of execution, honestly

The Apache-2.0 and CC-BY-4.0 halves have been in force since the repository
was first published. The reference-implementation half — the part earlier
revisions of this file described as "NOT yet executed" — is executed as of
this notice: the license texts are present at the repository root, each
crate declares its own license rather than inheriting a workspace default,
and first-party sources carry SPDX headers. Issue #133's open question
about generated code is answered above and recorded in D-016: it stays
Apache-2.0.

Three things are still worth knowing, and are stated rather than glossed:

  - "Sources" above means what it says, and no more: every first-party
    `.rs`, `.c`, `.h`, `.py`, `.sh` and `.js` file carries an inline SPDX
    header. Build manifests (`Cargo.toml`, `meson.build`), the IDL and its
    schema, Markdown, and test fixture data do not; those are covered by
    the path map above and, for crates, by the `license` field. A file
    without an inline header is not unlicensed — read the map.
  - Header coverage is not machine-checked. There is no `reuse lint`-style
    CI gate yet, so a new file added without an SPDX header will not be
    caught automatically. Adopting the REUSE Specification layout is the
    clean fix and is deferred to a follow-up, not silently assumed done.
  - GitHub's license detector reads the root LICENSE file and will keep
    reporting this repository as "Apache-2.0". That understates the
    copyleft half. This file, and the SPDX header on the file in front of
    you, are the accurate answer — the repository badge is not.

There is no MIT-licensed first-party code in this repository. Earlier
revisions of the shim's build file asserted a project-level MIT license; no
MIT license text ever accompanied it, and that assertion is retired by
D-016. Vendored dependencies under shim/subprojects/ do carry MIT and HPND
terms, as the third-party section above records.

Contributions are taken under the Developer Certificate of Origin
(sign-off), not a Contributor License Agreement — D-012. No copyright
assignment is asked for or accepted, and contributors keep their own
copyright.
